1. For each string, compute f(s) = frequency of the lexicographically smallest character.
2. For each query, count how many words have f(W) > f(query).

Approach:
- Compute f for all words, store in an array.
- Since string lengths are at most 10, f values are between 1 and 10.
- Build a frequency count array of f(word) values, then compute suffix sums so that for a given query value q, we can look up the number of words with f > q.
- For each query, compute f(queries[i]) and answer = count of words with f > q.

Implementation:

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] numSmallerByFrequency(String[] queries, String[] words) {
        int[] freq = new int[11]; // f values range 1..10
        for (String w : words) {
            freq[f(w)]++;
        }
        // suffix[i] = number of words with f >= i
        int[] suffix = new int[12];
        for (int i = 10; i >= 1; i--) {
            suffix[i] = suffix[i + 1] + freq[i];
        }
        int[] answer = new int[queries.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < queries.length; i++) {
            int q = f(queries[i]);
            answer[i] = suffix[q + 1];
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private int f(String s) {
        char min = 'z' + 1;
        int count = 0;
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c < min) {
                min = c;
                count = 1;
            } else if (c == min) {
                count++;
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

