The problem: find eventual safe states. Classic approach: reverse graph topological sort (Kahn's algorithm on reversed edges), or DFS with coloring (detect cycles).

Approach 1: DFS with three colors:
- 0 = unvisited, 1 = in progress (visiting), 2 = safe.
- If we encounter a node in "visiting" state, it's part of a cycle → unsafe.
- A node is safe if all its neighbors are safe.

Approach 2: Reverse the graph and do topological sort. Nodes with out-degree 0 are safe initially. Then repeatedly remove nodes whose all outgoing edges go to safe nodes.

Let me implement the reverse topological approach:

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.length;
        List<List<Integer>> reverseAdj = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) reverseAdj.add(new ArrayList<>());
        int[] outDegree = new int[n];
        for (int u = 0; u < n; u++) {
            outDegree[u] = graph[u].length;
            for (int v : graph[u]) {
                reverseAdj.get(v).add(u);
            }
        }
        Deque<Integer> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (outDegree[i] == 0) queue.offer(i);
        }
        boolean[] safe = new boolean[n];
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int node = queue.poll();
            safe[node] = true;
            for (int prev : reverseAdj.get(node)) {
                if (--outDegree[prev] == 0) queue.offer(prev);
            }
        }
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (safe[i]) result.add(i);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

This works. Since we iterate i from 0 to n-1, the result is sorted ascending.

