The problem is the classic Longest Increasing Subsequence. The O(n log n) solution uses patience sorting with binary search (tails array). Let me write it.

Approach: maintain a list `tails` where `tails[i]` is the smallest tail of an increasing subsequence of length i+1. For each num, binary search for the leftmost position where tails[pos] >= num (strictly increasing, so we use lower bound). If pos == size, append; else replace tails[pos] = num. The answer is the size of tails.

Let me write the code:

```java
class Solution {
    public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
        int[] tails = new int[nums.length];
        int size = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            int lo = 0, hi = size;
            while (lo < hi) {
                int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
                if (tails[mid] < num) {
                    lo = mid + 1;
                } else {
                    hi = mid;
                }
            }
            tails[lo] = num;
            if (lo == size) {
                size++;
            }
        }
        return size;
    }
}
```

