Looking at this problem:

**Approach:**
1. Sort gardens ascending. Gardens with `flowers[i] >= target` (a suffix of the sorted array) are already complete and can never become incomplete, so if all gardens are complete, answer is `n * full`.
2. For each possible number of complete gardens `k` (from `n - t` where `t` = count of gardens below target, up to `n`), it's optimal (exchange argument: for fixed k and min-level m, cost `a_i - b_i` is minimized by completing the k gardens with largest flower counts) to complete the `k` largest gardens. Cost = sum of `max(0, target - f_i)` over those.
3. With remaining budget, maximize the minimum of the remaining incomplete gardens, capped at `target - 1` (raising to `target` would make them complete — covered by a larger `k`). This is a monotone function of the level `m`, so binary search `m`, using prefix sums and a precomputed "first index with value >= m" array for O(1) cost evaluation.
4. `k = n` means no incomplete gardens → beauty `n * full`.

Verified against both examples (14 and 30) and several edge cases (already-complete gardens, huge budgets, partial-dominant vs full-dominant scenarios).

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public long maximumBeauty(int[] flowers, long newFlowers, int target, int full, int partial) {
        int n = flowers.length;
        Arrays.sort(flowers);
        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + flowers[i];
        }

        int lo = 0, hi = n;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = (lo + hi) >>> 1;
            if (flowers[mid] >= target) hi = mid;
            else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        int t = lo; // gardens [t, n-1] already have >= target flowers

        if (t == 0) {
            return (long) n * full;
        }

        int[] ub = new int[target + 1]; // ub[m] = first index with flowers[i] >= m
        int p = 0;
        for (int m = 0; m <= target; m++) {
            while (p < n && flowers[p] < m) p++;
            ub[m] = p;
        }

        long ans = 0;
        int startK = n - t;

        for (int k = startK; k <= n; k++) {
            int start = n - k; // incomplete gardens are indices [0, start-1]
            long cost;
            if (start >= t) {
                cost = 0;
            } else {
                cost = (long) (t - start) * target - (prefix[t] - prefix[start]);
            }
            if (cost > newFlowers) break;
            if (k == n) {
                long cand = (long) n * full;
                if (cand > ans) ans = cand;
                break;
            }
            long rem = newFlowers - cost;
            int mLo = 0, mHi = target - 1;
            while (mLo < mHi) {
                int mid = (mLo + mHi + 1) >>> 1;
                int q = ub[mid];
                if (q > start) q = start;
                long g = (long) q * mid - prefix[q];
                if (g <= rem) mLo = mid;
                else mHi = mid - 1;
            }
            long cand = (long) k * full + (long) mLo * partial;
            if (cand > ans) ans = cand;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
```

