This is the classic regular expression matching problem. The standard solution is dynamic programming.

Let me think through the DP approach:

Let `dp[i][j]` = true if the first `i` characters of `s` match the first `j` characters of `p`.

Base case: `dp[0][0] = true` (empty string matches empty pattern).

For `dp[0][j]`: empty string can match patterns like `a*`, `a*b*`, etc. So `dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-2]` if `p[j-1] == '*'`.

Transitions:
- If `p[j-1]` is a normal character or `.`:
  - `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] && (p[j-1] == s[i-1] || p[j-1] == '.')`
- If `p[j-1] == '*'`:
  - Zero occurrences: `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2]`
  - One or more occurrences: if `p[j-2] == s[i-1] || p[j-2] == '.'`, then `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i-1][j]`

Let me write the code:

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length(), n = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];
        dp[0][0] = true;

        // Handle patterns that can match empty string (like a*, a*b*)
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 2];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);
                if (pc == '*') {
                    // zero occurrences of p[j-2]
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2];
                    // one or more occurrences
                    char prev = p.charAt(j - 2);
                    if (prev == s.charAt(i - 1) || prev == '.') {
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i - 1][j];
                    }
                } else {
                    if (pc == s.charAt(i - 1) || pc == '.') {
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[m][n];
    }
}
```

Wait, there's a potential issue with `dp[0][j-2]` when `j == 1`. But the problem guarantees that each `*` has a previous valid character, so `p[0]` can never be `*`. So when `p[j-1] == '*'`, `j >= 2`, and `j - 2 >= 0`, so `dp[0][j-2]` is valid.

