Let me analyze this problem:

We have a 2D grid of size m x n. Each shift operation moves every element one position to the right (in the flattened row-major order, it's a circular shift by 1). So shifting k times is equivalent to a circular shift of the flattened array by k positions.

The flattened grid has m*n elements. Shifting right by k means element at flattened index i moves to index (i + k) % (m*n).

So the new grid at flattened position p gets the element from flattened position (p - k + m*n) % (m*n).

Let me write the solution:

```java
class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> shiftGrid(int[][] grid, int k) {
        int m = grid.length;
        int n = grid[0].length;
        int total = m * n;
        k = k % total;

        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        // Initialize result grid
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            List<Integer> row = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                row.add(0);
            }
            result.add(row);
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                int idx = i * n + j; // flattened index in original
                int newIdx = (idx + k) % total;
                int newRow = newIdx / n;
                int newCol = newIdx % n;
                result.get(newRow).set(newCol, grid[i][j]);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

