This is the classic Word Ladder problem. Standard BFS approach:

1. Put all words in a set.
2. If endWord not in set, return 0.
3. BFS from beginWord, at each step try all 26 character substitutions for each position, check if in set, remove from set (mark visited), add to queue.
4. Return level count when endWord found.

The answer counts words in sequence, so start count at 1 for beginWord.

Let me write it:

```java
class Solution {
    public int ladderLength(String beginWord, String endWord, List<String> wordList) {
        Set<String> set = new HashSet<>(wordList);
        if (!set.contains(endWord)) return 0;

        Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        queue.offer(beginWord);
        set.remove(beginWord); // avoid revisiting
        int steps = 1;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int size = queue.size();
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                String cur = queue.poll();
                if (cur.equals(endWord)) return steps;
                char[] chars = cur.toCharArray();
                for (int j = 0; j < chars.length; j++) {
                    char original = chars[j];
                    for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                        if (c == original) continue;
                        chars[j] = c;
                        String next = new String(chars);
                        if (set.remove(next)) {
                            queue.offer(next);
                        }
                    }
                    chars[j] = original;
                }
            }
            steps++;
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
```

This works. Complexity: O(N * L * 26) where N is number of words, L is word length.

Note: the "cur.equals(endWord)" check inside — but since we return steps when we pop it, and beginWord != endWord per constraints, it's fine. Alternatively check when adding to queue. Either works.

Edge: beginWord might equal a word in wordList; we remove it from set so no revisits.

Let me finalize.

